# text = It grew so dark , that the figures on the dial-plate were scarcely discernible ; but there the two old gentlemen continued to sit , in silence , with the watch between them In the obscure parlour of a low public-house , in the filthiest part of Little Saffron Hill ; a dark and gloomy den , where a flaring gas-light burnt all day in the winter-time ; and where no ray of sun ever shone in the summer : there sat , brooding over a little pewter measure and a small glass , strongly impregnated with the smell of liquor , a man in a velveteen coat , drab shorts , half-boots and stockings , whom even by that dim light no experienced agent of the police would have hesitated to recognise as Mr . William Sikes .
